# Create a custom portal

Declare the portal's app participant in native IDL, select the required Auth actions, and generate its TypeScript participant. Connect using that generated participant and the browser session-proof flow.

The configured login portal origin is the trusted portal security principal. A requesting application's origin is only a route selector and is not portal identity.

## Register the portal

Host the portal on a stable HTTPS origin. An administrator registers its entry
URL as a login portal and configures routes for the requesting applications.
Treat this as an authority change: a trusted portal authenticates users on behalf
of the runtime. Do not accept an arbitrary portal URL from an app's query string.

The portal participant needs only the Auth surfaces used by its login and
consent UI. Declare those selections in a `use required` block targeting the
locked Auth dependency in `trellis.toml`; run `trellis update` and
`trellis generate`. Import the generated participant rather than recreating
Auth requests or signed authorization objects.

## Complete the browser flow

The requesting app starts Trellis browser authentication and follows the
runtime-selected portal redirect. Preserve that flow identity throughout login,
OIDC continuation, consent, and denial. Use Trellis's browser helpers rather than
inventing a second session or passing an access token through the redirect URL.

Show the requesting application and requested authority before asking for
consent. Authenticate the user with the configured local or OIDC provider, then
complete the existing flow. A flow ID is not proof of browser possession:
browser binding and session-key proof are separate checks that the runtime owns.
Never treat `Origin` alone as authentication.

Handle expiry by starting a fresh flow, not by reusing an old approval URL.
Preserve provider errors as actionable user-facing failures, but do not show
credential material or signed proofs. Administrative authority cannot be
self-granted through an ordinary untrusted consent flow.

## Validate the integration

Use the built-in portal first to distinguish deployment/auth configuration from
custom UI errors. Then test your registered portal with a real app and runtime:
successful login, explicit denial, expired flow, and return to the requesting
app. Keep HTTPS, allowed origins, and callback URLs aligned across your reverse
proxy and runtime configuration. See [testing services](/guides/testing-trellis-services)
for real-runtime test ownership and [/api](/api) for the current Auth API.
